Why should we expect "LLM-assessed importance" to be relevant? This is just going to reflect existing discussions about mathematics, with unknown biases folded in.
LLMs are amazing at many things, but
holding novel and useful opinions
is not one of them (yet). They have incredible knowledge, speed and persistence, and that turns out to be enough to crack lots of problems that seemed very hard. But they don't have much in the way of good taste -- yet.
I strongly agree with Nate Silver's post from the other day (
https://www.natesilver.net/p/ai-is-moving-way-too-fast
), specifically:
while the LLM’s “research taste” is improving, it remains “mid”, not great. Our experience and domain knowledge,
for now,
remain highly important in building these models

It’s also pretty nice that a verb plus a table is implicitly a function call.  This means you can easily do things that seem to be types:
m = Menagerie {
Dog { tail, ears, snout=long },
Cat { you, get, the=idea },
table.unpack(ark),
}
